WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.319
A direct process API preserves argument
boundaries:

2
00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.597
each array element reaches the target
program as one argument rather than shell

3
00:00:07.677 --> 00:00:07.990
text.

4
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.702
Application code should own the array's
grammar.

5
00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:14.917
It chooses fixed option names, their
order, and which data positions may vary.

6
00:00:14.997 --> 00:00:17.002
A client may request an output format,

7
00:00:17.082 --> 00:00:21.280
but the server maps that choice to a known
format value and places it after a fixed

8
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:21.798
option.

9
00:00:21.878 --> 00:00:26.515
Forwarding a request-supplied string array
would instead expose the target utility's

10
00:00:26.595 --> 00:00:28.349
entire command-line interface.

11
00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:31.060
Validate each variable according to its
meaning.

12
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.210
Numeric settings need strict parsing and
bounded ranges.

13
00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.734
Format or mode choices belong to an
allowlist.

14
00:00:36.814 --> 00:00:41.200
Storage identifiers should map to
controlled server paths instead of becoming raw

15
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.157
path arguments.

16
00:00:42.237 --> 00:00:45.620
Do not assume TypeScript validates a body
cast to a string array;

17
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.768
type assertions disappear at runtime.

18
00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:52.171
The accepted request shape should be
parsed from unknown data and converted into a

19
00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:55.571
smaller internal operation before any
arguments are created.

20
00:00:55.651 --> 00:00:58.596
Argument separation prevents shell syntax
injection,

21
00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:01.182
but the target program performs another
parse.

22
00:01:01.262 --> 00:01:04.771
A file name beginning with a hyphen may be
interpreted as an option.

23
00:01:04.851 --> 00:01:07.795
Where the utility documents an
end-of-options marker,

24
00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:09.567
place it before operand values.

25
00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:13.970
Otherwise use an API or invocation form
that identifies operands unambiguously,

26
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.559
generate safe server-side file names, or
reject ambiguous input.

27
00:01:17.639 --> 00:01:20.395
Understand whether an option loads
configuration,

28
00:01:20.475 --> 00:01:24.485
writes to an arbitrary location, enables
plugins, changes network access,

29
00:01:24.565 --> 00:01:26.194
or interprets an input as code.

30
00:01:26.274 --> 00:01:28.655
Keep structural choices distinct from
data.

31
00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:33.434
Optional behavior should select from
application-authored argument fragments rather

32
00:01:33.514 --> 00:01:35.268
than accept pass-through flags.

33
00:01:35.348 --> 00:01:38.794
Fixed option-value pairs are easier to
audit than a generic list.

34
00:01:38.874 --> 00:01:41.568
Preserve ordering required by the target
program,

35
00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:45.533
and test values that resemble options even
when they are otherwise valid.

36
00:01:45.613 --> 00:01:48.495
The goal is not to let the client
construct argv safely.

37
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:52.835
The goal is to expose a small application
operation whose argv layout is fixed and

38
00:01:52.915 --> 00:01:55.860
whose variable operands are validated and
authorized.
